  <h1>Learn English step by step: a 12-week plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">Learning English step by step means one thing at a time, in the right order. This 12-week plan is for a beginner who can study about 30 minutes a day. By the end of it you will be able to introduce yourself, talk about your day, ask questions, and understand simple spoken English.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>Weeks 1–2 — First words and sounds</h2>
  <p>Learn the alphabet sounds, greetings, numbers 1–100, and your first 100 words: family, food, time, colours, everyday objects. Practise introducing yourself: “Hello, my name is… I am from… I live in…” Listen to slow English every day, even 10 minutes, and repeat what you hear.</p>
  <p>Your goal for week 2: say your name, where you are from, and three things you like — in English, without notes.</p>
  <h2>Weeks 3–4 — Present simple and everyday verbs</h2>
  <p>Learn the present simple: “I work”, “She works”, “They live here”. Learn the 20 most common verbs — be, have, go, do, say, get, make, know, think, take, see, come, want, use, find, give, tell, work, call, try — and use each one in a sentence about your own life.</p>
  <p>Start a daily diary: three sentences about your day, in English. It will be simple, and that is exactly right.</p>
  <h2>Weeks 5–6 — Questions, past simple and daily life</h2>
  <p>Learn to ask questions with do, does, did, what, where, when, why and how. Learn the past simple for finished actions: “I watched a film yesterday.” Talk about your day, your family, your job or studies, and your weekend plans.</p>
  <p>Your goal for week 6: have a 5-minute conversation about your day — past, present and future — even if it is slow.</p>
  <h2>Weeks 7–8 — Listening and understanding real English</h2>
  <p>Now push your listening: podcasts for learners, TV shows with English subtitles, and short news stories. Listen to the same clip twice — once for the main idea, once for the details. Write down five new words from each clip and use them in a sentence the same day.</p>
  <p>Read your first graded reader — a short, easy book written for learners. Reading a whole book, even a simple one, is a big confidence boost.</p>
  <h2>Weeks 9–10 — Speaking with confidence</h2>
  <p>Speak more, and speak about real things: your opinions, your plans, your problems. Practise the phrases people actually use: “What do you think?”, “I’m not sure”, “Could you say that again?” These small phrases make conversation flow.</p>
  <p>This is the best time to start lessons with a tutor. A tutor gives you someone to talk to, corrects your mistakes gently, and helps you say the things you actually want to say.</p>
  <h2>Weeks 11–12 — Review, test yourself, plan the next step</h2>
  <p>Review everything: go back through your notebook and test yourself on the words and grammar from weeks 1–10. Record yourself speaking for two minutes about your life, then listen to it. You will hear how far you have come.</p>
  <p>Then set your next goal: it might be a longer conversation, a job interview in English, or an exam like IELTS. A clear next goal is what keeps you learning after the 12 weeks end.</p>
  <h2>Your 30-minute daily routine</h2>
  <p>Here is a routine that works for the whole 12 weeks: 10 minutes of vocabulary (new words plus review), 10 minutes of listening or reading, and 10 minutes of speaking or writing. If you only have 15 minutes, do 5 of each. The routine matters more than the length — a short daily habit beats a long session once a week.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Skipping ahead to advanced grammar before the basics are solid.</li>
    <li>Studying for hours one day and nothing for the rest of the week.</li>
    <li>Only learning new words and never reviewing the old ones.</li>
    <li>Waiting until your English is “perfect” before speaking — it never will be, and that is fine.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Follow the order: words, then simple grammar, then listening, then speaking.</li>
    <li>Keep the 30-minute routine, even on busy days — shorten it, do not skip it.</li>
    <li>Write down your mistakes and the correct version next to them.</li>
    <li>Celebrate small wins: your first conversation, your first book, your first film without subtitles.</li>
  </ul>

