  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How much English will I know after 12 weeks?</summary><p>You will know roughly 600–1,000 words and the core grammar for everyday topics: introducing yourself, your day, your family, your plans, asking questions and talking about the past. You will not be fluent — but you will be able to use English in real situations, which is the foundation everything else is built on.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I need to study every day?</summary><p>Short and daily works far better than long and rare. Your brain stores new language while you sleep, so a little every day builds much faster than one big session a week. If you miss a day, just start again the next day — missing one day does not undo your progress.</p></details>
  <details><summary>What should I do after the 12 weeks?</summary><p>Keep the routine and raise the level: longer conversations, harder reading, and a clear goal like work, travel or an exam. Many learners start regular tutor lessons at this point, because conversation practice is what turns studied English into usable English.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is 30 minutes a day enough?</summary><p>Yes, for steady progress. Thirty focused minutes a day adds up to more than 3 hours a week — and focused study counts double. If you can do more on some days, great, but never let a long session replace the daily habit.</p></details>
